        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-207(1), a definite and seasonable expression of acceptance operates as an acceptance even though it states terms additional to or different from those offered. Under UCC § 2-207(2), between merchants, additional terms become part of the contract UNLESS: (a) the offer expressly limits acceptance to the terms of the offer; (b) they MATERIALLY ALTER it; or (c) notification of objection to them has already been given or is given within a reasonable time. Official Comment 4 to UCC § 2-207 explicitly provides that a clause requiring mandatory binding arbitration or waiving a party's constitutional right to a jury trial in court is a quintessential MATERIAL ALTERATION as a matter of law. A material alteration does not become part of the contract through mere silence or failure to object; it drops out unless the offeror expressly assents. The arbitration clause is not part of the contract (Option C). Option A is incorrect because UCC § 2-207 explicitly abolished the common law mirror image rule and last-shot doctrine. Option B is incorrect because additional terms that materially alter the contract do NOT automatically become part of the contract between merchants. Option D is incorrect because an acknowledgment operates as an acceptance unless made expressly conditional on assent to the new terms."
