        exp: "Rule: Under the common law of justifiable self-defense, an actor is privileged to use deadly force ONLY IF the actor HONESTLY AND REASONABLY BELIEVES that such force is necessary to prevent imminent death or great bodily harm to himself. Self-defense requires BOTH subjective belief and objective reasonableness. Because Defendant was an expert gunsmith who instantly recognized that the flintlock pistol was completely incapable of firing, Defendant lacked an honest, subjective belief that he was in imminent danger of death or great bodily harm. Instead, Defendant used the encounter as a pretext to execute an old enemy with express malice aforethought. Self-defense is completely unavailable (Option B). Options A and C are incorrect because self-defense requires a genuine subjective belief in peril; an actor who knows he is in zero danger cannot claim self-defense. Option D is incorrect because imperfect self-defense requires an *honest* (though unreasonable) belief in the necessity of force; Defendant had no subjective belief whatsoever that deadly force was necessary."
